ker of perfusion  and it can predict a bad outcome but  really  the patient needs to be in at least  occult sepsis for it to be elevated and  has very poor specificity  for bacterial infection that's not due  to that that doesn't lead to sepsis  so procalcitonin the advantages here are  much more specific for bacterial  infection  um it it is uh  has favorable kinetics again that rise  in half-life can correlate with severity  of illness  and it can help guide antibiotic use and  you can sort of monitor the  levels over time there is however a need  for education  there's a need for lab instrumentation  and it is more expensive than  white count crp and lactate  i showed this slide just to highlight  that clinicians are often very concerned  about sensitivity and specificity but  what we really want is a negative  predictive value  and of course that's going to depend on  the prevalence of the bacterial  infection in the population  that you're looking at however  procalcitonin levels can be used as a  rapid tool to rule out bacteremia  given the high number of false positive  blood cultures and the time it takes to  detect bacterial infection with a blood  culture  this is a study that was published in  jama in 2009 the pro house study  it was a multi-center non-inferiority  trial that was in  six tertiary care eds in switzerland and  they looked at approximately four  fourteen hundred patients um and what  they looked at was  um lower respiratory tract infections  they looked at antibiotic  with um use with a pct algorithm versus  the standard of care  and really had similar outcomes with no  change in adverse events and what they  were able to show  was a decreased duration of antibiotic  use in these patients  not insignificant from 8.7 to 5.7 days  here's another meta-analysis looking at  procalcitonin use  in 11 trials comprising 40 000  plus patients for lower respiratory  tract infection and the pct guided  patients compared to the  usual care had lower odds of antibiotic  initiation and a shorter mean antibiotic  use  there was no adverse impact on mortality  or length of stay  that's actually a good thing you want to  show that there's not an increase in  adverse events  from using these strategies  in the intensive care unit a  meta-analysis of rcts and adult sepsis  showed that pct guided patients  did have shorter antibiotic duration  compared with controls  about you know slightly less than a day  but not insignificant for icu patients  and pro-calcitin use again had no  adverse impact on mortality  finally there was a patient-level  randomized control trial  of hospitalized patients with lower  respiratory tract infection  from the ed in 14 us hospitals and i'm  going to show this study just to to show  that sometimes even though you have  a good test the failure of  implementation  may lead to less than desirable outcomes  so in this case  they randomized patients to usual care  versus clinical guidance for the role of  pct with an algorithm and then they  didn't find any significant differences  in antibiotic days between the two  groups  so why is that well a lot of the sites  were not  particularly comfortable with this  algorithm and so the education piece was  a challenge  there were also some challenges with  interpreting the algorithm and their  assays with different cutoffs  and the basis of calculating sensitivity  and specificity of pct levels as a  circuit marker for bacterial infection  really  um you know we need better diagnostic  tests for lower respiratory tract  infections  um and typically these patients were of  the sickest so they were  patients that were in the ed that had  community acquired pneumonia that were  being admitted to the hospital so the  clinician  already made the decision that this  patient needed an antibiotic and so in  this case  the pct may not have swayed them  in terms of delaying starting those  antibiotics  although the results of not having any  impact on duration were also a little  bit surprising  so why might this be perhaps some of you  have read this book thinking fast and  slow i think it really highlights how an  emergency clinician thinks  but really we have two distinct  cognitive systems we have a more  automatic system and a more reflective  system so people like to think that  clinicians are deliberate and rational  and reflective when they're making  clinical decisions  however in reality because we're humans  and we try to save our brain  space and energy a lot of decisions are  actually automatic so they're  uncontrolled effortless  based on associations they're quick and  they may also be experience based and  they're not conscious  so those are the ones that we really try  to target  to guide clinicians or nudge clinicians  to making better decisions  so what's the solution to this problem  of implementation  well at least in the research realm  implementation science based approaches  that are based on the principles of  behavioral economics can improve both  test ordering and clinical decision  making  so essentially engineering choices and  systems to give us the biggest bang for  the buck for new tests  some of this work can be done through  site-specific guidelines that  incorporate these biomarkers into  clinical practice guidelines or clinical  pathways that make it easier for  clinicians  to make decisions based on algorithms  there's an opportunity to tailor  recommendations based on individual  facility  antimicrobial susceptibility such as  from the antidiagram or formulary  there's an opportunity to provide  provider feedback  and comparisons and the role for  appropriate diagnostics can be  facilitated by clinical decision support  and informatics tools and we are having  some success in this area  at this time but what we really need is  a paradigm shift  we need novel diagnostics and algorithms  to aid in clinical decision making and  these need to  be incorporated into our workflows and  take into account  clinical decision making we also have a  debate about  early targeted versus empiric antibiotic  use  we really want all of us in the end to  improve patient outcomes and decrease  adverse events  and a nice benefit would also be the  downstream public health benefit of  decreased antimicrobial resistance in  the future  and preserving the antibiotics we have  so some future directions might include  more rapid  and cost effective diagnostic tools that  include the host response  such as biomarkers like pct  we really don't want to incorporate  tests that are going to require a lot of  training  and in the ed at least when we consider  point of care or even in laboratories  that have limited space  we really have to think about the  footprint of the instruments as well  so with that i will take any questions  and that is my email if anyone has any  interest in reaching out after this  presentation  thank you for your encounter  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you would like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  okay let's get started our first  question  is  how will pct be used during flu season  especially during the  current covid19 pandemic  so actually we're doing some research at  our institution around  um pct versus usual care with a clinical  pharmacist intervention to help guide  appropriate antiviral therapy  for patients with influenza and reduce  the inappropriate use  of antimicrobials um where they're not  indicated  i think you know with covid uh this has  really impacted  you know our testing strategies right  now the main thing that people are  focused on is the diagnosis of covid  um and you know supplemented perhaps by  imaging in our in our sicker patients  i think you know we find that pct is  typically very low  um in covid as in other viral infe