ing ordered are not  actually being used in the way they were  intended so i think again that workflow  and implementation  and getting the clinicians to change  their behavior is the frustration on the  part of infectious diseases  but i will say that a test alone without  incorporating antimicrobial stewardship  or other strategies  is probably not going to lead to the  desired outcome so  i encourage everyone who's thinking  about implementing these biomarkers or  other  rapid diagnostic tests to think about  how the clinicians will use those  results  and really trying to drive practice  change so that you can actually see the  outcomes that you want  [Music]  makes sense thank you so much here we go  our next question  do you use the same cut off  procalcitonin value for infants as  adults  um so i believe the cutoffs may be  slightly different i don't have them up  in front of me but we have an algorithm  for adults and an algorithm for kids and  then of course  you know depending if it's an lrti or or  sepsis but those are included in  in the lab order so that we can we can  see those  um when interpreting the results  in fact there wasn't a lot of data i  should say when we first  started trying to use pct for instance  there wasn't a lot of data so  you know many many institutions when we  talked to our pediatric id colleagues  you know they had to come up with their  own algorithm based on other  institutions because  many many pediatric hospitals weren't  actually really using this so  it made it a little challenging when we  started this  and you stated pct utilization is an  educational intensive process  who is best who's the best driver of  bringing pct  to hospitals  so i think ultimately a lot of the  decision making  at least at our institution may be that  enter my critical stewardship  team so we're very fortunate here at uc  davis we have a very good relationship  between our stewardship team that  incorporates um  the laboratory and incorporate